    {
      "entryId": "mal-4-5",
      "file": "bookThreadDetails",
      "field": "principle",
      "text": "God promises to send Elijah the prophet before the coming of the great and dreadful day of the LORD to turn the hearts of fathers to children and children to fathers. Gabriel announces that John the Baptist will minister \"in the spirit and power of Elijah,\" and Jesus explicitly confirms John was the Elijah of the first advent. In biblical and historicist prophecy, an Elijah-message of reformation also precedes the second advent. First principle: reformational preaching must restore covenant fidelity and family order before divine judgment.",
      "density": 3.9214,
      "words": 86,
      "sentences": 5,
      "maxSentenceWords": 31,
      "gate": "PASS",
      "violations": [],
      "context": {
        "citations": []
      }
    },
    {
      "entryId": "mal-4-2",
      "file": "bookThreadDetails",
      "field": "principle",
      "text": "But unto you that fear my name shall the Sun of righteousness arise with healing in his wings; and ye shall go forth, and grow up as calves of the stall. For the wicked the day burns; for the fearing it dawns. First principle: the same coming is noon-darkness to one and sunrise to the other — the dividing line is the fear of His name.",
      "density": 3.4214,
      "words": 66,
      "sentences": 3,
      "maxSentenceWords": 31,
      "gate": "PASS",
      "violations": [],
      "context": {
        "citations": []
      }
    },
    {
      "entryId": "mal-3-1",
      "file": "bookThreadDetails",
      "field": "principle",
      "text": "God promises to send His messenger to prepare the way before Him, and the Lord whom they seek will suddenly come to His temple, even the Messenger of the Covenant. Jesus identifies John the Baptist as this messenger, and Himself as the Lord visiting His temple. First principle: divine visitation is preceded by a purifying prophetic message calling the covenant people to repentance.",
      "density": 3.2893,
      "words": 63,
      "sentences": 3,
      "maxSentenceWords": 30,
      "gate": "PASS",
      "violations": [],
      "context": {
        "citations": []
      }
    },
    {
      "entryId": "mal-3-8",
      "file": "bookThreadDetails",
      "field": "principle",
      "text": "Will a man rob God? Yet ye have robbed Me. But ye say, Wherein have we robbed Thee? In tithes and offerings. God claims the tithe (ten percent of increase) as holy unto Himself. Returning tithes and offerings into the storehouse acknowledges God as the true Owner of all creation and funds the worldwide proclamation of the gospel without worldly commercialism.",
      "density": 3.0679,
      "words": 61,
      "sentences": 6,
      "maxSentenceWords": 27,
      "gate": "PASS",
      "violations": [],
      "context": {
        "citations": []
      }
    },
    {
      "entryId": "mal-3-17",
      "file": "bookThreadDetails",
      "field": "principle",
      "text": "Jewels made up; spared as a father spares a serving son. First principle: the making-up of jewels is a day-event — the spared are named as a father spares his serving son.",
